WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.439 --> 00:00:30.290
You are probably familiar with the musical
‘Evita,’ by Tim Rice and Andrew Lloyd

00:00:30.290 --> 00:00:35.850
Webber, which tells the story of Eva Duarte
and her relationship with Argentinian President

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:36.850
Juan Perón.

00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:41.390
In the most famous number, ‘Don’t Cry
for Me, Argentina’, Evita addresses the

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:46.090
crowds after orchestrating an insurrection
to free her husband, prisoner to a military

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:47.090
Junta.

00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:51.130
A young Che Guevara witnesses the events,
providing some commentary – in song form,

00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:52.130
of course.

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:57.899
How much of this famed musical scene is true,
and how much is just dramatized rumours?

00:00:57.899 --> 00:01:04.180
We’ll find out together in todays’ Biographics,
which covers the lives of Evita and Juan Perón,

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:09.280
two illegitimate children who rose from obscurity
to ultimate power.

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:17.500
Evita Discovers Her Talent
Maria Eva Duarte, later known simply as Eva

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:24.190
or Evita, was born on May 7, 1919, in the
town of Los Toldos, in the Argentine pampas,

00:01:24.190 --> 00:01:25.920
or fertile lowlands.

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:31.100
She was the youngest of five children born
to Juana Ibarguren and Juan Duarte.

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:35.240
Juan and Juana were not married; in fact,
he had a second, ‘official’ family in

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:36.240
the town of Chivilcoy.

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:40.670
Juan was a wealthy and successful farm manager,
with close ties to rich land owners and the

00:01:40.670 --> 00:01:42.890
elite members of the Conservative party.

00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:47.140
After Eva’s birth, this party started losing
ground to the Radicals, which caused a decline

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:48.520
in Juan’s fortunes.

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:52.040
The Duartes, accustomed to relative wealth,
had to tighten the belt.

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:56.700
A few years later, they had to punch some
extra holes in the belt altogether, when Juan

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:59.960
died in a car accident on January 8, 1926.

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:04.640
Juan Duarte had never recognised the children
he had with Juana, meaning they would receive

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:05.840
no inheritance.

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:10.590
This humiliation was compounded by the treatment
received at Juan’s funeral: his lawful widow

00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:15.020
and her wealthy family physically prevented
Evita and her siblings from attending the

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:16.020
service.

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:20.870
This was a watershed moment in Evita’s upbringing:
the knowledge that she was an explicit part

00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:25.220
of the lower rungs of Argentinian society,
ostracised by the rich elite.

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:31.070
By the time Eva started first grade, her older
siblings Juan Jr and Elisa were already working

00:02:31.070 --> 00:02:36.690
as clerks, while mom Juana spent hours on
her sewing machine as a self-employed seamstress.

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:41.510
Eva was not yet burdened with these responsibilities
and would spend her school-free hours climbing

00:02:41.510 --> 00:02:44.200
trees and exploring the countryside.

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:49.030
Perhaps most importantly, she enjoyed dressing
up and improvising plays for her friends and

00:02:49.030 --> 00:02:50.030
siblings.

00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:55.010
Her co-star was second-youngest sister Erminda,
and they could count on Juan Jr as a stage

00:02:55.010 --> 00:02:56.010
designer.

00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:57.010
The boy even built a miniature circus for
them.

00:02:57.010 --> 00:02:59.810
In 1930, the Duartes moved to the town of
Junín.

00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:05.150
It was here that Eva’s penchant for dress-up
games evolved into a full-blown passion for

00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:09.090
acting, with a rich diet of films watched
at the village cinema and radio dramas.

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:13.110
She also joined a school drama group and performed
for the first time on stage.

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:18.990
By 1934, at age 15, Eva made her choice: she
would move to Buenos Aires and become a professional

00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:19.990
actress.

00:03:19.990 --> 00:03:22.520
Her mother Juana was not sold on the idea.

00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:26.940
How could a girl so young face the dangers
of the big capital?

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:31.150
According to some accounts, it was celebrity
Tango singer Augustín Magaldi who convinced

00:03:31.150 --> 00:03:33.560
Juana to let Eva follow her dreams.

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:38.940
In the Rice/Webber musical, the much older
Magaldi is portrayed as Eva’s lover, and

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:40.560
the two elope to Buenos Aires.

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:45.599
A less dramatic but more likely vision of
events comes courtesy of Eva’s older sister,

00:03:45.599 --> 00:03:49.380
Erminda: it was the headmaster of Eva’s
school who had noticed the girl’s talent

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:53.960
and convinced mom Juana to let the girl press
her fortune with acting.

00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:57.890
And before you start gossiping, no, Eva did
not elope with the headmaster.

00:03:57.890 --> 00:04:00.050
There was no eloping here.

00:04:00.050 --> 00:04:04.110
It was Juana who took Evita to Buenos Aires
and arranged for her to move in with some

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:05.880
family friends.

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:08.020
The adventure had begun.

00:04:08.020 --> 00:04:16.440
Big Break in the Big City
Argentina in the mid-1930s was a nation struggling

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:21.950
under the weight of poverty, unemployment
and hunger – just as much of the world did,

00:04:21.950 --> 00:04:24.240
following the financial collapse of 1929.

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:29.120
Tens of thousands of Argentines moved from
the farms and countryside into Buenos Aires,

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:32.160
looking for work and better living conditions.

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:35.930
Year after year, the social gap widened between
the urban middle and upper classes, and the

00:04:35.930 --> 00:04:37.310
desperately poor.

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:42.070
This was the backdrop for Eva’s first steps
in professional acting, as she, too, struggled

00:04:42.070 --> 00:04:44.520
to make a living amidst fierce competition.

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:49.670
But she clearly had talent, brains and beauty,
which is always a powerful combination.

00:04:49.670 --> 00:04:54.770
Eva was soon hired by the Compania Argentina
de Comedias, a professional troupe, and made

00:04:54.770 --> 00:04:57.770
her debut in March 1935.

00:04:57.770 --> 00:05:02.370
In 1937, Eva turned 18 and her career was
taking off: she first appeared on the silver

00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:03.960
screen and then on the radio.

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:09.340
While her roles on stage and in cinema were
mainly bit parts or supporting characters,

00:05:09.340 --> 00:05:14.710
it was radio where Eva could get her best
parts and progress her career.

00:05:14.710 --> 00:05:19.040
And it was radio, not cinema, that made her
a household name to Argentinians.

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:24.210
A co-star in one of her many radio dramas
complimented Eva, labeling her as professional,

00:05:24.210 --> 00:05:25.330
dependable, and talented.

00:05:25.330 --> 00:05:31.060
But Eva also picked up a reputation as, well,
not exactly the nicest person to be around.

00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:34.620
It seems that Eva was generally not liked
many of her colleagues.

00:05:34.620 --> 00:05:38.419
It may have been these same colleagues who
later spread rumours that Eva’s career was

00:05:38.419 --> 00:05:41.560
accelerated by a propensity to sleep with
the right people.

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:46.650
It is more likely, though, that these rumours
were circulated after she had become Mrs Perón,

00:05:46.650 --> 00:05:48.930
in order to undermine the President.

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:51.620
Neither him, nor Evita, would ever lack enemies.

00:05:51.620 --> 00:05:56.250
Whatever the truth, Eva Duarte surely did
not lack in talent, as she was routinely signed

00:05:56.250 --> 00:05:58.660
up for one radio drama after the other.

00:05:58.660 --> 00:06:04.300
In 1943, Eva reached the peak of her popularity
as the star of - I am not joking here - a

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:05.300
biography show!

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:06.300
Yes!

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:08.450
That’s why I do what I do:
Step 1 – host a biography show

00:06:08.450 --> 00:06:11.889
Step 2 – get to run a country
Step 3 – have a musical named after me.

00:06:11.889 --> 00:06:13.143
[Editing note: a poster in the style of a
1970s musical show appears on screen.

00:06:13.143 --> 00:06:14.143
The title is
‘Simonito’

00:06:14.143 --> 00:06:15.143
By Tim Shell and Andrew Lloyd Shell.

00:06:15.143 --> 00:06:16.143
If this doesn’t take me to hell, I don’t
know what else.]

00:06:16.143 --> 00:06:19.580
Jokes aside, Eva’s show was a collection
of dramatizations in which she played the

00:06:19.580 --> 00:06:25.430
role of women who had made history, like Elizabeth
I, Catherine the Great, Isadora Duncan, and

00:06:25.430 --> 00:06:27.020
Sarah Bernhardt, among others.

00:06:27.020 --> 00:06:31.509
I wonder if she could imagine that one day,
her own life story could have been dramatized

00:06:31.509 --> 00:06:36.210
in such a show …
1943 was a year of importance, not just to

00:06:36.210 --> 00:06:37.690
Eva, but to all Argentinians.

00:06:37.690 --> 00:06:42.150
On June 4, a military coup led by General
Pedro Ramirez seized power from President

00:06:42.150 --> 00:06:43.800
Ramón Castillo.

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:47.259
Within the group of conspirators, there was
an ambitious Colonel, who was put in charge

00:06:47.259 --> 00:06:49.670
of the Ministry of Labour and Welfare.

00:06:49.670 --> 00:06:59.070
His name was Colonel Juan Perón.

00:06:59.070 --> 00:07:01.580
The Boxing Minister
Juan Domingo Perón was born in Los Lobos,

00:07:01.580 --> 00:07:05.520
province of Buenos Aires, on October 8, 1895.

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:11.140
Like Eva, Juan was born out of wedlock, the
child of wealthy farmer Mario Tomás Perón

00:07:11.140 --> 00:07:12.419
and Juana Sosa.

00:07:12.419 --> 00:07:17.900
Juan spent most of his childhood and early
teenage years in the pampas in the south of

00:07:17.900 --> 00:07:18.900
the country.

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:21.360
He always wished to return to the capital
to study medicine, but when he did return

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:24.800
to Buenos Aires, it was to enter the military
academy in 1911.

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:31.150
He graduated in 1913, barely eighteen, with
the rank of second lieutenant in the infantry.

00:07:31.150 --> 00:07:36.389
Juan was amongst the first of his class, and
he gained a reputation as a hard-working student.

00:07:36.389 --> 00:07:41.479
He also excelled in physical training: standing
at 6-foot-tall and solidly built, the young,

00:07:41.479 --> 00:07:45.990
athletic Juan became an Army champion in boxing,
skiing, and fencing.

00:07:45.990 --> 00:07:49.020
He almost made it onto the Argentine Olympic
fencing team!

00:07:49.020 --> 00:07:53.490
As Argentina never entered a war during his
early military career, Perón only knew about

00:07:53.490 --> 00:07:54.690
peace-time deployment.

00:07:54.690 --> 00:07:58.440
He progressed through the ranks thanks to
his academic endeavours, as well as the publication

00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:01.289
of several books on military morals and hygiene.

00:08:01.289 --> 00:08:05.030
In 1925, Juan first met with 17-year old Aurelia
Tizón.

00:08:05.030 --> 00:08:09.340
After a four-year engagement, the two married
on January 5, 1929.

00:08:09.340 --> 00:08:12.690
Aurelia was a teacher, intelligent and academically
minded.

00:08:12.690 --> 00:08:17.600
A valuable companion for Perón, Aurelia dedicated
much of her time to translating military theory

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:20.389
textbooks from English for her husband.

00:08:20.389 --> 00:08:24.800
In 1930, the now-Captain Perón was a member
of the Army Staff, as well as a Professor

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:27.830
of Military History at the Higher Institute
for War Studies.

00:08:27.830 --> 00:08:32.740
He continued publishing military textbooks
and expanded his field of knowledge into linguistics,

00:08:32.740 --> 00:08:37.630
with a book on the language of the Araucanos,
an indigenous people from in and around Patagonia.

00:08:37.630 --> 00:08:42.180
In 1936, Perón had become a Major and was
tasked with a delicate assignment.

00:08:42.180 --> 00:08:47.810
Officially, he was detached as a military
attaché to the Argentinian Embassy in Chile;

00:08:47.810 --> 00:08:53.339
unofficially, Juan was actually spying on
the Chilean Army, in search of potential plans

00:08:53.339 --> 00:08:58.130
for an invasion of Argentina, which was an
event feared to be likely at that time.

00:08:58.130 --> 00:09:03.350
Perón was a skilled agent, reporting on Chile’s
military capabilities without ever blowing

00:09:03.350 --> 00:09:04.410
his cover.

00:09:04.410 --> 00:09:08.630
This success may have earned him his next
promotion, to Lieutenant-Colonel, at the end

00:09:08.630 --> 00:09:09.920
of the same year.

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:15.320
On September 10, 1938, after a prolonged period
of illness, Aurelia died young, at the age

00:09:15.320 --> 00:09:16.320
of 30.

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:18.240
The diagnosis was cervical cancer.

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:20.600
This was a devastating blow to the officer.

00:09:20.600 --> 00:09:25.130
A valued asset to Argentina’s military,
Perón’s state of depression worried his

00:09:25.130 --> 00:09:28.990
superiors, to the point that they offered
him another post abroad to get his mind off

00:09:28.990 --> 00:09:29.990
the tragedy.

00:09:29.990 --> 00:09:34.750
From 1939 to the beginning of 1940, Perón
was attached to the army’s European Mission

00:09:34.750 --> 00:09:35.960
of Foreign Studies.

00:09:35.960 --> 00:09:41.930
This involved him touring Italy, Spain, Germany,
Hungary, and France to study and observe those

00:09:41.930 --> 00:09:42.930
countries’ militaries.

00:09:42.930 --> 00:09:47.510
He spent most of his time in Italy: here,
Perón focused on mountain warfare, but he

00:09:47.510 --> 00:09:53.610
also had the chance to study closely the policies
and authoritarian methods of Benito Mussolini.

00:09:53.610 --> 00:09:58.790
While never fully adhering to Fascist ideology,
Perón came to appreciate the dictatorial

00:09:58.790 --> 00:10:03.000
methods in use in Italy and other European
countries.

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:09.040
He started to appreciate the notion of strong,
direct, authoritarian rule as the only means

00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:11.519
to impose effective reforms in his home country.

00:10:11.519 --> 00:10:12.519
During this period Perón also had the opportunity
to experience the early German victories in

00:10:12.519 --> 00:10:13.519
Western and Northern Europe.

00:10:13.519 --> 00:10:14.519
This convinced him that the Axis would eventually
emerge victorious from the conflict and shaped

00:10:14.519 --> 00:10:15.519
his future policies.

00:10:15.519 --> 00:10:16.930
Juan Perón returned to Argentina in 1941,
having risen to the rank of Colonel.

00:10:16.930 --> 00:10:22.310
The country at that time was ruled by President
Ramón Castillo, whose term had become unpopular

00:10:22.310 --> 00:10:27.110
due to widespread corruption, fraud, economic
stagnation, and an increasing divide between

00:10:27.110 --> 00:10:28.720
the upper and lower classes.

00:10:28.720 --> 00:10:31.410
This is when Perón started getting involved
in politics.

00:10:31.410 --> 00:10:38.230
He joined a secret outfit called GOU – Grupo
Oficiales Unidos, or Group of United Officers

00:10:38.230 --> 00:10:40.680
– which had a nationalist agenda.

00:10:40.680 --> 00:10:45.670
In July 1943 the GOU seized power in an almost
bloodless coup.

00:10:45.670 --> 00:10:50.110
General Arturo Rawson took power, in roughly
the amount of time needed to choose the colour

00:10:50.110 --> 00:10:52.070
scheme for his office curtains.

00:10:52.070 --> 00:10:57.529
Rawson was favourable to the Allied cause
in WWII and was open to civilians in his cabinet,

00:10:57.529 --> 00:11:01.209
but his fellow conspirators disagreed with
both ideas.

00:11:01.209 --> 00:11:06.540
Rawson was promptly replaced with General
Pedro Ramirez, whose agenda and methods were

00:11:06.540 --> 00:11:12.250
much more closely aligned to the Axis Powers
-- firmly anti-Leftist and totalitarian.

00:11:12.250 --> 00:11:17.899
Ramirez appointed a General Farrell as Minister
of War, who brought along Perón as his personal

00:11:17.899 --> 00:11:18.899
secretary.

00:11:18.899 --> 00:11:22.740
This was not enough for the ambitious Colonel,
who volunteered for a post nobody wanted:

00:11:22.740 --> 00:11:25.260
that of Minister of the National Labour Department.

00:11:25.260 --> 00:12:01.810
It wasn’t just a move to get a seat

00:12:01.810 --> 00:12:24.709
in

00:12:24.709 --> 00:12:53.140
the cabinet, but a calculated first step in

00:12:53.140 --> 00:13:03.430
a long-term plan.

00:13:03.430 --> 00:13:10.220
Earthquake
Minister Perón delivered an astounding amount

00:13:10.220 --> 00:13:14.519
of reform in the span of two years, all of
which gained him the support of the masses

00:13:14.519 --> 00:13:16.010
of industrial workers.

00:13:16.010 --> 00:13:20.760
For example, he introduced paid holidays for
all workers; he also limited work hours and

00:13:20.760 --> 00:13:22.640
mandated better working conditions.

00:13:22.640 --> 00:13:28.209
Perón also established a system of government-affiliated
trade unions to defend workers’ rights and

00:13:28.209 --> 00:13:31.390
always intervened on their side against industry
owners.

00:13:31.390 --> 00:13:36.290
He did not refrain from using government funds
to pay for worker salaries or pensions when

00:13:36.290 --> 00:13:38.730
factory owners threatened to suspend payments.

00:13:38.730 --> 00:13:43.620
These progressive policies certainly benefitted
the lower classes, which made Perón increasingly

00:13:43.620 --> 00:13:44.620
popular.

00:13:44.620 --> 00:13:48.420
This was his strategy: bolster the status
of the workers, the majority of the population,

00:13:48.420 --> 00:13:51.940
which would grant him support and even devotion
in return.

00:13:51.940 --> 00:13:56.220
Perón referred to his supporters as the ‘descamisados’,
or the ‘shirtless.’

00:13:56.220 --> 00:14:00.149
No Argentinian was under the illusion that
Perón operated within a democracy.

00:14:00.149 --> 00:14:02.990
This was a military, authoritarian, government.

00:14:02.990 --> 00:14:06.530
But unlike previous democratic cabinets, Perón
delivered.

00:14:06.530 --> 00:14:12.070
On the January 15, 1944, a national emergency
put Perón to the test: an earthquake completely

00:14:12.070 --> 00:14:17.480
obliterated the province of San Juan, killing
7000 inhabitants and injuring another 12,000.

00:14:17.480 --> 00:14:22.300
Perón organised a nationwide campaign to
raise funds in aid of the wounded and the

00:14:22.300 --> 00:14:23.300
displaced.

00:14:23.300 --> 00:14:27.820
For this effort, he drafted national celebrities
to assist him, including popular actors from

00:14:27.820 --> 00:14:30.269
the stage, cinema, and radio.

00:14:30.269 --> 00:14:34.670
Some days later, Eva set up a meeting with
Juan in his office, during which she asked

00:14:34.670 --> 00:14:38.010
for more opportunities to raise funds for
the earthquake victims.

00:14:38.010 --> 00:14:41.550
It was then that Perón felt an immediate
attraction for the young actress.

00:14:41.550 --> 00:14:44.780
He later recalled:
“As I looked at her, I felt that her words

00:14:44.780 --> 00:14:50.010
were taking over me: I was almost subjugated
by the warmth in her voice and in her eyes”

00:14:50.010 --> 00:14:54.649
And just like that, an expert of coups de
theatre and a veteran of a coup d’état,

00:14:54.649 --> 00:14:58.430
were brought together by a coup de foudre
– if you’ll pardon my French.

00:14:58.430 --> 00:15:01.220
The two initially kept their relationship
a secret.

00:15:01.220 --> 00:15:02.220
Why?

00:15:02.220 --> 00:15:07.290
The age difference may have played a part,
as he was 49 and she was 25.

00:15:07.290 --> 00:15:09.050
It was their own damn business after all.

00:15:09.050 --> 00:15:12.269
Besides their romantic lives, also their careers
were fluorishing.

00:15:12.269 --> 00:15:17.890
In early 1944, Perón’s patron Gen Farrell
became President, and he appointed his protegé

00:15:17.890 --> 00:15:21.450
as Vice President and Minister of War, on
top of his Labour duties.

00:15:21.450 --> 00:15:25.580
Eva was no less overworked, hosting three
daily radio shows: one on biographies, one

00:15:25.580 --> 00:15:28.950
on geography and one on lists of 10 interesting
items.

00:15:28.950 --> 00:15:29.950
Okay, fine.

00:15:29.950 --> 00:15:34.070
It was actually two radio dramas and a propaganda
broadcast in support of the regime.

00:15:34.070 --> 00:15:38.440
On the July 9, 1944, the two lovebirds decided
to go public with their relationship by showing

00:15:38.440 --> 00:15:40.040
up together at a gala event.

00:15:40.040 --> 00:15:44.089
Perón had become the most popular member
of the regime, enjoying great visibility with

00:15:44.089 --> 00:15:49.889
the press, and so did Evita, appearing by
his side in public.

00:15:49.889 --> 00:15:57.260
Don’t Cry for Me
In March of 1945, President Farrell opportunistically

00:15:57.260 --> 00:16:02.360
declared war on the Axis powers, securing
a place in the United Nations for Argentina.

00:16:02.360 --> 00:16:06.959
In August, he decided to soften his regime,
even promising free elections by the end of

00:16:06.959 --> 00:16:07.959
the year.

00:16:07.959 --> 00:16:11.050
In the meantime, Perón had fallen out of
favour with the political establishment.

00:16:11.050 --> 00:16:15.610
His populist reforms had alienated the support
of the more traditional elements of the ruling

00:16:15.610 --> 00:16:17.630
classes, the military, and the church.

00:16:17.630 --> 00:16:22.980
Evita had also come under attack, accused
of being only a power hungry social climber,

00:16:22.980 --> 00:16:24.660
no more than a prostitute.

00:16:24.660 --> 00:16:29.190
The Power Couple’s downward descent was
prologue to Perón’s appointment of a friend

00:16:29.190 --> 00:16:32.470
of Evita’s as head of the Department of
Posts and Telegraph.

00:16:32.470 --> 00:16:37.579
Perón was forced to resign on October 10,
1945; by the 13th, he was arrested on the

00:16:37.579 --> 00:16:39.920
Island of Martín García.

00:16:39.920 --> 00:16:44.529
Years later, Perón recalled how Evita immediately
organised a general labor strike, followed

00:16:44.529 --> 00:16:47.300
by a massive demonstration of the descamisados.

00:16:47.300 --> 00:16:51.570
She marched at the head of hundreds of thousands
of workers, occupying Plaza de Mayo in Buenos

00:16:51.570 --> 00:16:54.839
Aires, the square in front of the seat of
government.

00:16:54.839 --> 00:16:57.420
Evita then addressed the crowds from a balcony.

00:16:57.420 --> 00:16:59.330
A young Che Guevara was among them.

00:16:59.330 --> 00:17:02.730
This was remembered as the Foundation Day
of Peronismo.

00:17:02.730 --> 00:17:03.990
Or not.

00:17:03.990 --> 00:17:10.199
Juan Perón is the only source to ever circulate
this version of the events, which was later

00:17:10.199 --> 00:17:12.100
incorporated in the musical.

00:17:12.100 --> 00:17:15.679
In reality, Evita likely did not play a role
in the uprising at all.

00:17:15.679 --> 00:17:19.139
The strike was spontaneous, as were the mass
demonstrations.

00:17:19.139 --> 00:17:23.609
The descamisados rallied in Plaza de Mayo,
demanding for Perón to be released.

00:17:23.609 --> 00:17:27.939
The authorities, hoping to prevent bloodshed,
complied with their demands, asking Perón

00:17:27.939 --> 00:17:30.549
to step onto the balcony to calm down the
mob.

00:17:30.549 --> 00:17:32.049
And how about Guevara?

00:17:32.049 --> 00:17:37.740
He was 17 at that time, still attending secondary
school, and did not move to Buenos Aires until

00:17:37.740 --> 00:17:38.740
1947.

00:17:38.740 --> 00:17:43.289
He never met Evita; their only recorded contact
is a letter he wrote to her, asking if she

00:17:43.289 --> 00:17:45.389
could buy him a jeep.

00:17:45.389 --> 00:17:50.039
As soon as Perón was released from prison,
he did two things: he resigned from the military,

00:17:50.039 --> 00:17:54.580
leaving with the rank of General, and he married
Evita in a registry office, on the 22nd of

00:17:54.580 --> 00:17:58.369
October, followed by a religious ceremony
on the 10th of December.

00:17:58.369 --> 00:18:03.080
With Eva by his side, Perón set off to organise
his candidature in the new general elections.

00:18:03.080 --> 00:18:07.729
He ran as a candidate from the Partido Laborista,
or Labour Party.

00:18:07.729 --> 00:18:12.610
His opposition, the Democratic Union, was
a disparate coalition that included both conservatives

00:18:12.610 --> 00:18:15.999
and communists, with the open backing US Ambassador
Spruille Braden.

00:18:15.999 --> 00:18:21.950
His influence was so extensive that the electoral
choice was often presented as ‘Perón vs

00:18:21.950 --> 00:18:22.950
Braden’.

00:18:22.950 --> 00:18:27.899
Juan and Evita organised an intense campaign
trail with a chartered train, called “El

00:18:27.899 --> 00:18:28.899
Descamisado”.

00:18:28.899 --> 00:18:33.109
This was the first time in the history of
Argentina that a presidential candidate was

00:18:33.109 --> 00:18:34.399
accompanied by his wife.

00:18:34.399 --> 00:18:40.159
On February 8, 1946, she pioneered another
‘first’: at a party rally, she replaced

00:18:40.159 --> 00:18:43.480
Juan on the speaker’s podium, as he was
too ill to speak.

00:18:43.480 --> 00:18:46.190
Unfortunately, that was a total disaster.

00:18:46.190 --> 00:18:50.039
While workers loved Perón thanks to his earlier
reforms, they were still mistrustful of his

00:18:50.039 --> 00:18:51.039
wife.

00:18:51.039 --> 00:18:55.299
The crowd would not let her speak, drowning
her out with cries of “We want Perón!”

00:18:55.299 --> 00:18:58.899
But little by little, Evita grew in popularity.

00:18:58.899 --> 00:19:03.919
Her acting talent, trained voice, and overall
good looks helped in no small measure.

00:19:03.919 --> 00:19:09.210
On June 4, 1946, the couple found their victory:
Juan Perón had been democratically elected

00:19:09.210 --> 00:19:17.250
the new President of Argentina, and Evita
had become first lady at 27 years of age.

00:19:17.250 --> 00:19:23.600
Justicialismo
Juan Perón’s Presidency is not easily defined,

00:19:23.600 --> 00:19:26.119
if not as a labyrinth of contradictions.

00:19:26.119 --> 00:19:30.909
Let’s begin with his ideology, known as
‘justicialismo’, or ‘justicialism’:

00:19:30.909 --> 00:19:35.630
it was founded on social justice and heavy
state intervention in the form of welfare

00:19:35.630 --> 00:19:37.859
programs to support the lower classes.

00:19:37.859 --> 00:19:42.150
But it was also known as ‘Perónismo’,
an indication that this movement was a unique

00:19:42.150 --> 00:19:47.220
way of running a country, centred around the
cult of Juan Perón’s personality and strong

00:19:47.220 --> 00:19:48.220
leadership.

00:19:48.220 --> 00:19:52.919
Juan Perón introduced several progressive
reforms, mainly to the benefit of his electoral

00:19:52.919 --> 00:19:54.679
base, the working classes.

00:19:54.679 --> 00:19:58.820
He made social security, education, and healthcare
totally free and universal.

00:19:58.820 --> 00:20:02.549
He introduced paid maternity leave and the
right to vote for women, with Evita’s key

00:20:02.549 --> 00:20:03.549
contribution.

00:20:03.549 --> 00:20:08.039
Perón also boosted the Argentine economy
by developing hydroelectric power plants,

00:20:08.039 --> 00:20:13.000
launching a local iron and steel industry,
and expanding shipbuilding by 500 percent.

00:20:13.000 --> 00:20:17.320
But to achieve all this, he implemented rash
authoritarian measures, including censorship

00:20:17.320 --> 00:20:19.890
of the press and incarceration of dissenters.

00:20:19.890 --> 00:20:25.149
In terms of foreign policy, Perón’s strategy
for Argentina was to become a neutral country

00:20:25.149 --> 00:20:29.280
along a ‘third position,’ aligned with
neither capitalism nor communism during the

00:20:29.280 --> 00:20:30.280
Cold War.

00:20:30.280 --> 00:20:35.470
In practice, Perón did not hide his hostility
towards American and British presence or influence

00:20:35.470 --> 00:20:36.649
over Argentina.

00:20:36.649 --> 00:20:39.650
Was he secretly considering a drift towards
Moscow?

00:20:39.650 --> 00:20:41.250
Not exactly.

00:20:41.250 --> 00:20:45.980
After becoming President, Juan Perón made
Argentina a well-known haven for former Nazis.

00:20:45.980 --> 00:20:52.139
Adolph Eichmann, Josef Mengele, Erich Priebke,
Otto Skorzeny, and many, many other war criminals

00:20:52.139 --> 00:20:53.629
all found refuge there.

00:20:53.629 --> 00:20:58.499
Skorzeny even became a bodyguard to the Peróns,
and may have had a liaison with Evita – a

00:20:58.499 --> 00:21:00.409
rumour that was never confirmed.

00:21:00.409 --> 00:21:05.559
All of these Nazi officials, except for Skorzeny,
had been sentenced in absentia at the Nuremberg

00:21:05.559 --> 00:21:08.369
trials and were wanted for crimes against
humanity.

00:21:08.369 --> 00:21:12.659
Perón was doing more than just accepting
them into his country, here -- he actively

00:21:12.659 --> 00:21:16.090
invited them, offering money, houses, and
jobs.

00:21:16.090 --> 00:21:17.700
What was his plan then?

00:21:17.700 --> 00:21:22.659
Perón had estimated that a new World War
would break out by 1949, fought by blocs led

00:21:22.659 --> 00:21:24.549
by the US and the Soviet Union.

00:21:24.549 --> 00:21:28.739
His end strategy was to bargain for Argentina’s
participation on the Western side.

00:21:28.739 --> 00:21:33.749
And that’s when the expertise of ex-Nazis
would have come handy, as trained officers

00:21:33.749 --> 00:21:36.580
adept at fighting the Soviets.

00:21:36.580 --> 00:21:44.330
No Sleep for Evita
In June 1947 Evita had her first official

00:21:44.330 --> 00:21:48.070
trip abroad, the ‘Rainbow Tour’ across
Europe and South America.

00:21:48.070 --> 00:21:53.289
Her first leg of the tour was in Spain, where
she met Generalisimo Francisco Franco, considered

00:21:53.289 --> 00:21:56.850
to be one of the last vestiges of Fascism
in Europe.

00:21:56.850 --> 00:21:59.299
This did not help the image of the Peróns.

00:21:59.299 --> 00:22:04.039
In subsequent visits to Italy and Switzerland,
Evita was accused of being the representative

00:22:04.039 --> 00:22:09.649
of a Fascist country and was booed, or even
pelted with eggs and rotten fruit.

00:22:09.649 --> 00:22:12.639
During her official visit to London, she was
snubbed by the King.

00:22:12.639 --> 00:22:16.869
Upon returning from Europe, Evita dedicated
herself to the cause of women’s suffrage.

00:22:16.869 --> 00:22:22.340
She had already supported a bill to that effect
in 1946, and now she wanted things to change,

00:22:22.340 --> 00:22:23.970
and fast.

00:22:23.970 --> 00:22:29.090
Evita launched an appeal to Argentinian women,
who staged two massive rallies in September

00:22:29.090 --> 00:22:32.869
of 1947 and plastered Buenos Aires with posters.

00:22:32.869 --> 00:22:38.559
On the September 23, Parliament ratified the
bill: suffrage was truly universal in Argentina.

00:22:38.559 --> 00:22:43.190
Evita capped the achievement two years later
by founding the first all-female Party in

00:22:43.190 --> 00:22:47.399
Argentina, to ensure that women would be on
the ballot, instead of just at the ballot.

00:22:47.399 --> 00:22:51.659
Evita understood her feminist victory would
not have been possible without the backing

00:22:51.659 --> 00:22:54.049
and active participation of her husband.

00:22:54.049 --> 00:22:57.929
In her writing, she made it clear that in
order to bring about progress, both male and

00:22:57.929 --> 00:22:59.830
female action were necessary.

00:22:59.830 --> 00:23:03.320
She summarised this as:
“A man of action triumphs over everyone

00:23:03.320 --> 00:23:04.320
else.

00:23:04.320 --> 00:23:08.979
A woman of action triumphs for everyone else”
In other words: ‘male action’ was needed

00:23:08.979 --> 00:23:14.259
to overcome opposition when imposing new ideas,
but ‘female action’ was a constructive

00:23:14.259 --> 00:23:18.960
energy, the one to really bring those new
ideas to the benefit of others.

00:23:18.960 --> 00:23:24.499
Inspired by these ideals, Evita threw herself
into Welfare work: by the end of 1947 she

00:23:24.499 --> 00:23:29.009
had become a sort of unofficial Minister of
Labour, committing to a gruelling work schedule

00:23:29.009 --> 00:23:34.519
of up to 22 hours a day, meeting trade union
delegations and addressing welfare issues.

00:23:34.519 --> 00:23:39.519
Examples of her work included organising State-funded
holiday camps for workers’ children, building

00:23:39.519 --> 00:23:43.899
hospitals for textile industry employees,
and distributing aid to the poorest families

00:23:43.899 --> 00:23:44.899
in the country.

00:23:44.899 --> 00:23:49.349
And because she had two hours left each day,
Evita put that time to good use.

00:23:49.349 --> 00:23:54.349
To complement her aid efforts, she created
the Foundation Eva Perón, focused on helping

00:23:54.349 --> 00:23:56.399
women, children and the elderly.

00:23:56.399 --> 00:24:00.710
she collected funds mainly as donations from
Trade Unions and channelled them into several

00:24:00.710 --> 00:24:03.969
initiatives, such as the construction of schools
and hospitals.

00:24:03.969 --> 00:24:08.359
The Foundation continued to finance and develop
major works in the field of education and

00:24:08.359 --> 00:24:12.440
healthcare during the early 1950s, with Evita
supervising most of them.

00:24:12.440 --> 00:24:17.200
At this stage, even Perón supporters could
not help wondering where the required funds

00:24:17.200 --> 00:24:18.200
came from.

00:24:18.200 --> 00:24:22.940
Officially, these came from voluntary donations
by Trade Unions, as well private enterprises.

00:24:22.940 --> 00:24:28.200
But there was strong suspicion that these
donations were enforced by threats: either

00:24:28.200 --> 00:24:29.929
donate, or face the consequences.

00:24:29.929 --> 00:24:33.119
Take, for example, the candy factory called
‘Mu-mu’.

00:24:33.119 --> 00:24:37.239
The company was forcibly shut down by the
Government, with all its employees losing

00:24:37.239 --> 00:24:38.239
their job.

00:24:38.239 --> 00:24:42.749
Allegedly, it was because of poor hygienic
conditions inside the factory, but Evita’s

00:24:42.749 --> 00:24:47.070
direct involvement in a subsequent meeting
between factory management and trade unions

00:24:47.070 --> 00:24:52.399
led to suspicions that Mu-mu was being punished
for failing to donate to the Foundation.

00:24:52.399 --> 00:25:01.090
No stops for Juan
In 1949, Juan Perón succeeded in having a

00:25:01.090 --> 00:25:03.570
new Constitution ratified by parliament.

00:25:03.570 --> 00:25:09.200
This new text included a new round of reforms,
such as universal suffrage, and even a declaration

00:25:09.200 --> 00:25:12.909
of the rights of the elderly, drafted by Evita’s
foundation.

00:25:12.909 --> 00:25:17.570
But the main aim of the constitution was to
allow for Juan to run for a second term in

00:25:17.570 --> 00:25:19.100
future elections.

00:25:19.100 --> 00:25:22.919
As was often the case, Evita would play a
key role by his side.

00:25:22.919 --> 00:25:28.090
This time, it would have been a more explicit,
institutionalized one: in August 1951, the

00:25:28.090 --> 00:25:33.259
trade unions formally asked Juan Perón to
run again for President, this time with Evita

00:25:33.259 --> 00:25:34.669
as his Vice-President.

00:25:34.669 --> 00:25:38.219
Despite popular enthusiasm for the idea, Evita
eventually declined.

00:25:38.219 --> 00:25:42.309
This may have been due to pressure from military
leadership, who always disliked the First

00:25:42.309 --> 00:25:43.309
Lady.

00:25:43.309 --> 00:25:45.690
Another reason may have been Evita’s own
poor health.

00:25:45.690 --> 00:25:49.419
She had been suffering from fatigue, frequent
fainting, and vaginal bleeding.

00:25:49.419 --> 00:25:54.249
Today, we know that all these symptoms pointed
to a diagnosis of cervical cancer – the

00:25:54.249 --> 00:25:57.029
same illness that had killed Perón’s first
wife.

00:25:57.029 --> 00:26:01.529
But the President had decided to hide the
truth from Evita, for reasons that are still

00:26:01.529 --> 00:26:02.529
unclear.

00:26:02.529 --> 00:26:06.379
Perhaps it was to shield her from a devastating
diagnosis, or perhaps he just wanted to hide

00:26:06.379 --> 00:26:07.809
the truth from the public.

00:26:07.809 --> 00:26:13.039
In a cynical electoral move, Perón did not
want to reveal that Evita -- perhaps the biggest

00:26:13.039 --> 00:26:18.080
source of his presidential popularity -- may
not even be alive during his second term in

00:26:18.080 --> 00:26:19.080
office.

00:26:19.080 --> 00:26:23.480
Juan Perón won the elections in November
of 1951, with a 62 percent majority.

00:26:23.480 --> 00:26:25.989
Evita appeared by his side as he was sworn
in.

00:26:25.989 --> 00:26:31.789
It was one of her last public appearances.

00:26:31.789 --> 00:26:37.009
The Death of Evita
Evita Perón’s last months on Earth were

00:26:37.009 --> 00:26:38.009
painful.

00:26:38.009 --> 00:26:42.130
The official version of her death, on the
26th of July 1952, is that this was caused

00:26:42.130 --> 00:26:44.179
by late-stage cervical cancer.

00:26:44.179 --> 00:26:48.679
It may not be a coincidence that Perón’s
first wife Aurelia had suffered the same fate.

00:26:48.679 --> 00:26:54.690
One of the causes of cervical cancer is infection
by HPV – the human papilloma virus.

00:26:54.690 --> 00:27:00.071
It is likely that Juan Perón was a carrier
of such virus and had thus infected both of

00:27:00.071 --> 00:27:01.940
his wives.

00:27:01.940 --> 00:27:07.309
In 2015, a new explanation for Evita’s demise
was put forward by Dr Daniel Nijensohn, a

00:27:07.309 --> 00:27:10.440
neurosurgeon at Yale University Medical School.

00:27:10.440 --> 00:27:14.289
Nijensohn had obtained scans of Evita’s
skull after her death, which showed signs

00:27:14.289 --> 00:27:15.679
of being drilled into.

00:27:15.679 --> 00:27:19.769
This was evidence that the First Lady had
received a lobotomy -- an operation that involves

00:27:19.769 --> 00:27:24.099
cutting the neural connections between the
prefrontal lobes and the rest of the brain

00:27:24.099 --> 00:27:26.519
in order to numb emotional responses.

00:27:26.519 --> 00:27:31.590
One possibility is that the lobotomy had been
a radical measure to manage the pain of her

00:27:31.590 --> 00:27:36.859
cancer; a more sinister one is that Juan had
ordered the lobotomy to curb Evita’s increasingly

00:27:36.859 --> 00:27:38.309
unpredictable behaviour.

00:27:38.309 --> 00:27:42.259
As she succumbed to her cancer, Evita’s
actions were becoming more and more extreme.

00:27:42.259 --> 00:27:46.729
In her last months of life, Evita called for
violent action against her enemies and the

00:27:46.729 --> 00:27:51.610
enemies of the people, which she identified
as the oligarchs in traditional Argentinian

00:27:51.610 --> 00:27:52.610
society.

00:27:52.610 --> 00:27:56.710
It appears that she even ordered from her
sick bed a consignment of 5,000 automatic

00:27:56.710 --> 00:28:00.700
pistols and 1,500 machine guns to arm the
trade unions.

00:28:00.700 --> 00:28:03.950
She was seriously risking the onset of a civil
war.

00:28:03.950 --> 00:28:08.639
The news would have been enough to tear apart
the factions of Juan Peron’s allies, who

00:28:08.639 --> 00:28:11.509
already objected to Evita’s power and popularity.

00:28:11.509 --> 00:28:14.799
The country could have soon descended into
conflict.

00:28:14.799 --> 00:28:20.059
According to Dr Nijensohn, the lobotomy was
performed by an American surgeon, James Poppen,

00:28:20.059 --> 00:28:21.330
within the Presidential palace.

00:28:21.330 --> 00:28:24.669
Poppen’s nurse, Manena Riquelme, reported
that Poppen had first practiced the operation

00:28:24.669 --> 00:28:27.830
on convicts taken from Buenos Aires’ prisons.

00:28:27.830 --> 00:28:33.009
Medically, the intervention was at least a
partial success, as Evita did not die immediately.

00:28:33.009 --> 00:28:35.769
It also succeeded in silencing her for good.

00:28:35.769 --> 00:28:39.080
The lobotomy put her in a catatonic state,
and she stopped eating.

00:28:39.080 --> 00:28:44.799
Her health quickly deteriorated, until her
death in July -- eight month after Perón

00:28:44.799 --> 00:28:46.080
had been reelected -- at age 33.

00:28:46.080 --> 00:28:50.119
The masses mobilised one last time for her
funeral, with thousands of citizens following

00:28:50.119 --> 00:28:51.119
the procession.

00:28:51.119 --> 00:28:55.259
Evita’s body was placed at the headquarters
of the General Labour Confederation, while

00:28:55.259 --> 00:28:57.599
a publicly funded mausoleum was being built.

00:28:57.599 --> 00:29:00.940
Juan Perón had ordered that her body be perfectly
embalmed.

00:29:00.940 --> 00:29:06.479
Her body thus became a sort of relic, to be
adored by the masses – a fitting end, considering

00:29:06.479 --> 00:29:13.029
that the Pope immediately started receiving
requests for Evita to become a saint.

00:29:13.029 --> 00:29:19.559
Life After Evita
By late 1952, Juan Perón realised that he

00:29:19.559 --> 00:29:22.849
could not continue his policies of public
spending forever.

00:29:22.849 --> 00:29:25.940
The country’s finances were sluggish, and
the economy was stagnating.

00:29:25.940 --> 00:29:31.229
Moreover, the death of Evita had dealt a hard
blow to his popularity, exacerbated by rumours

00:29:31.229 --> 00:29:33.990
that he had started dating a 14 year old girl.

00:29:33.990 --> 00:29:36.889
Catholic authorities, and the military, were
not happy.

00:29:36.889 --> 00:29:41.009
Perón changed his foreign policy in an effort
to revive the economy.

00:29:41.009 --> 00:29:45.309
Traditionally anti-American, he made a U-turn
and looked for a rapprochement with Washington,

00:29:45.309 --> 00:29:49.049
a move that should have revived Argentina’s
exports to the US.

00:29:49.049 --> 00:29:53.799
But it was only a matter of time before a
new conspiracy ended his tenure in office.

00:29:53.799 --> 00:29:59.669
This happened on the 16th of September 1955:
a coup led by the more traditionalist echelons

00:29:59.669 --> 00:30:05.009
of the Navy and the Air Force took power,
after shelling Plaza de Mayo and causing 400

00:30:05.009 --> 00:30:06.009
casualties.

00:30:06.009 --> 00:30:08.710
Perón was forced into exile by the new military
Junta.

00:30:08.710 --> 00:30:12.279
He first moved to Paraguay and, five years
later, landed in Madrid.

00:30:12.279 --> 00:30:16.460
By the time he was in Spain, he’d taken
a third wife, Maria Estela Martinez, who was

00:30:16.460 --> 00:30:19.190
an Argentinian dancer better known as “Isabel.”

00:30:19.190 --> 00:30:22.200
In the meantime, the Junta had forbidden all
aspects of Perón’s policies of Justicialismo

00:30:22.200 --> 00:30:23.200
and Peronismo.

00:30:23.200 --> 00:30:26.229
Displaying images of Juan Perón and Evita
was also forbidden.

00:30:26.229 --> 00:30:30.899
This ban extended to Evita’s body -- in
1955, it was removed from its mausoleum.

00:30:30.899 --> 00:30:34.179
No one can account for her body in the ensuing
16 years.

00:30:34.179 --> 00:30:38.049
Some cynical soul once said that the ex you
can’t compete with is a dead one.

00:30:38.049 --> 00:30:40.440
Isabel Martinez certainly must have felt that
way.

00:30:40.440 --> 00:30:44.830
As she and Juan led a life of luxury in Madrid,
former president Perón never stopped looking

00:30:44.830 --> 00:30:47.049
for Evita’s embalmed remains.

00:30:47.049 --> 00:30:52.469
In 1971, out of nowhere, came the revelation
that Evita had been secretly buried in a cemetery

00:30:52.469 --> 00:30:55.609
in Milan, under a headstone bearing a different
name.

00:30:55.609 --> 00:31:00.159
Juan Perón recovered her body, had it restored
by a mortician and kept it for some days in

00:31:00.159 --> 00:31:01.230
his Madrid home.

00:31:01.230 --> 00:31:04.759
Not just anywhere -- laying on a table in
his living room.

00:31:04.759 --> 00:31:09.379
And Isabel would be asked to brush Evita’s
hair every day, to make sure it did not tangle.

00:31:09.379 --> 00:31:11.539
I will not comment on this, though I’m sure
you will.

00:31:11.539 --> 00:31:13.959
Back to normality.

00:31:13.959 --> 00:31:19.080
In 1973, public pressure in Argentina demanded
for Perón to return from his exile.

00:31:19.080 --> 00:31:23.469
The President in charge, General Lanusse,
complied, and invited Juan to return to the

00:31:23.469 --> 00:31:28.289
homeland, so Juan and Isabel moved back to
Buenos Aires on June 20, 1973.

00:31:28.289 --> 00:31:32.690
Lanusse had also called for general elections
to take place in March – but had made a

00:31:32.690 --> 00:31:35.599
point that Perón should not be allowed to
run.

00:31:35.599 --> 00:31:39.009
This was not a problem, as he participated
and won by proxy!

00:31:39.009 --> 00:31:44.399
A revived Justicialist Party put forward another
candidate, Mr Cámpora, who won with a thin

00:31:44.399 --> 00:31:46.849
majority of 49.59 percent.

00:31:46.849 --> 00:31:51.429
After taking power, Cámpora immediately resigned,
initiating new elections in September … which,

00:31:51.429 --> 00:31:53.889
of course, allowed for Perón to participate.

00:31:53.889 --> 00:31:54.899
Sly old fox.

00:31:54.899 --> 00:31:58.879
Juan and Isabel Perón campaigned together
as President and Vice-President, respectively,

00:31:58.879 --> 00:32:01.580
scoring a 60 percent victory by the end of
the year.

00:32:01.580 --> 00:32:06.239
Juan had become President of Argentina for
the third time, with his wife as second-in-command.

00:32:06.239 --> 00:32:10.519
Isabel had succeeded in gaining the vice presidency,
a post that would have perfectly suited Evita

00:32:10.519 --> 00:32:12.539
if it weren’t for her illness.

00:32:12.539 --> 00:32:15.791
Perón’s third term was short-lived, though,
as he died of a heart attack on July 1, 1974,

00:32:15.791 --> 00:32:17.370
at the age of 78.

00:32:17.370 --> 00:32:20.299
Isabel succeeded him as President, the first
female head of government in the Western Hemisphere.

00:32:20.299 --> 00:32:21.299
Legacies
The legacy of Evita and Juan Perón still

00:32:21.299 --> 00:32:22.299
live on today.

00:32:22.299 --> 00:32:23.299
Argentina’s power couple have been at turns
described as genuine champions of the descamisados,

00:32:23.299 --> 00:32:24.299
or as cynical, selfish and opportunistic power
grabbers.

00:32:24.299 --> 00:32:25.299
Whatever their intentions, the results are
undeniable: according to political scientist

00:32:25.299 --> 00:32:26.299
Peter H. Smith it was Peronism who gave the
Argentine lower classes a feeling of significance

00:32:26.299 --> 00:32:27.299
and strength, a sense of identity.

00:32:27.299 --> 00:32:28.299
Perón may have brought the powerless into
politics simply to build a power base, but

00:32:28.299 --> 00:32:29.299
the working class, women and unions remained
important political actors.

00:32:29.299 --> 00:32:30.299
After the Peróns, various regimes came to
power, some of them violently authoritarian.

00:32:30.299 --> 00:32:31.299
Regardless of their policies, the Peróns’
legacy is what allowed the mobilized masses

00:32:31.299 --> 00:32:32.299
to remain active and assert their rights,
even in the most turbulent of times … but

00:32:32.299 --> 00:32:33.299
that’s another story, for another day.

00:32:33.299 --> 00:32:34.299
Well I hoped you liked today’s super long
video, the story of this couple was so interesting

00:32:34.299 --> 00:32:35.299
that it deserved some extra time.

00:32:35.299 --> 00:32:36.299
Feel free to share like etc etc … and a
final question from me: is there any other

00:32:36.299 --> 00:32:54.469
historical character from a musical show that
you would like us to cover?

